My guest today is Dr. Robin Kowalski, a centennial professor of psychology at Clemson University, where she studies complaining and other aversive behaviors such as teasing and bullying. She has authored more than 100 journal articles and book chapters and several books, including complaining, teasing, and other annoying behaviors. Her research on complaining has been featured on the Today Show and in the Wall Street Journal, The Washington Post, and the New York Times. In addition to doing research, she has received many honors for her teaching, including Clemson's Phil Prince Award for Excellence in Innovation in the Classroom, and a mention in the Princeton Reviews list of the 300 best professors in the nation. Dr. Kualski, thank you for joining me today.

5
00:02:21.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Thank you so much for having me. I'm excited to be here. Good. Well, let's start with the big question. Why do we complain so much? What's the purpose? Well, everybody does it. So it has to have a function. And I think there's a lot of different reasons why people complain, albeit to different degrees. I think probably the most common reason we complain is just for catharsis, you know, just to get our dissatisfactions off our chest. It just makes us feel better. Even if, let's say, it's a person that we're dissatisfied with, even if they can't hear us, it still makes us feel better to do it. So I think that's the most common function. I think it also allows us to get information. So it really serves what we call in my field a social comparison function. You know, if I complain about the weather, I may be wanting to get somebody else's perception of, is it really as hot as I think it is? Or maybe it's just me. Maybe I'm getting sick. You know, maybe there's something wrong with me. Students, students oftentimes will complain about a professor, about how hard a test was, because they want to get a gauge of, was it really that hard? Maybe I just wasn't prepared for it.

6
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:50.000
So I think we oftentimes complain about something to get a response from other people. I also think we complain to get other people to account for their behavior. So, you know, we might complain, you know, if our, you know, spouse is always late getting home from work, for example. We might be like, you know, why are you always late getting home? And oftentimes has that wine attached to it. But, you know, we're wanting them to offer an explanation, you know, were they at the gym again? Or did they stay at work late? What was the reason?

7
00:03:50.000 --> 00:04:27.000
So we complain about the behavior in order for them to offer us an explanation. You've identified two different types of complaining, right? Expressive complaining, which is just venting, right, which you've described, and instrumental complaining. So what are the differences? So expressive complaining is exactly what you said. It's just that complaining, that cathartic complaining, just to get it off our chest. Instrumental complaining is complaining that's designed to bring about a very specific function. You know, a lot of your retail complaining, you know, if we buy a product and we're dissatisfied with that product and then we go to the company and say, you know, my new vacuum cleaner is not working. You know, there's something wrong with it. Then that's

8
00:04:27.000 --> 00:05:10.000
instrumental complaining because we want them to either fix it or to just, you know, trade it in for a different one that hopefully will work. So it's intended to bring about a very specific outcome. Now, you wrote an article in psychology today called complaining how to do it with finesse. So let's talk about that. How can people complain more effectively and what separates an effective instrumental complaint from one that just alienates people? So I think there's a couple of key components to this. One, I think we need to complain in moderation. And two, I think we need to complain strategically. You know, everybody complains to different degrees. You know, you've got some people who complain infrequently, although they still complain some. And then you've got people, some people who

9
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:48.000
complain all the time. But you really need to complain in moderation. If you complain, you know, excessively, you're going to wear out your audience. You know, your friends are going to tired of it and they're not going to want to spend time with you. And it's just, it's wearing. It also can make other people aware of their own dissatisfaction and maybe they don't want to be made aware of that. So again, it creates a distancing in relationships. So you need to complain in moderation. I think the second component to it though is you really need to do it strategically. You need to pick your audience carefully. I mean, the truth is, if you have a problem with a product or service, you should complain to the manufacturer about it or go back to the store. You know,

10
00:05:48.000 --> 00:06:37.000
I'm incessantly dissatisfied with my internet service. But, you know, me, me venting about that to my neighbors, while it makes me feel better, so on the expressive end, I'm doing better, on the instrumental end, it's still not going to rectify any problem I have with my internet service. Whereas if I call the company up and I'm like, okay, here's my list, and it's usually a list of things that, you know, it's too slow, it's not working, it's this, it's that, then again, presumably the company will do something to rectify that situation. So that's being stream. You know, it's not just venting. It's hopefully in moderation. It depends on how bad the service is. And it's doing it strategically. Is expressive complaining, which we've already described as just venting, is that good for our emotional and mental health? Or does it sometimes just make us feel worse?

11
00:06:37.000 --> 00:07:17.000
I think it's both. I think it depends on how often you do it. You know, I based a lot of my theorizing, I guess, on complaining on Dr. Jamie Pennebaker's research on disclosure of traumatic events. And I'm not saying that complaining is the same as disclosing a traumatic event. But his research showed that if you disclose a traumatic event that you've never disclosed before, it produces improved physical and psychological health. So to your question, you know, I think if we don't complain all the time, if we don't complain incessantly, again, if we do it in moderation and we're strategic about it. And so we're inhibiting sometimes, I think that's when you get the benefit of complaining. You're going to get, you know, maybe initially your affect will be negative

12
00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:47.000
because you're thinking about what you're dissatisfied with. But in time, once you express it and kind of are thinking about your dissatisfaction, you know, sometimes it's when it's in our head. It's more muddled that it is when we actually express it and get it out. But if you complain all the time and you're never inhibiting, then where's the benefit? Where's the, you know, all the Pena Baker's research, there's got to be some inhibition to get the release of. And again, I'm not equating, complaining with the disclosure of traumatic events.

13
00:07:47.000 --> 00:08:14.000
But I think there's some similarities in terms of that release of the inhibition, release of the dissatisfaction. And I think that's when you get the benefit. Is there a relationship between complaining and happiness? In other words, do people who complain more tend to be more or less happy than people who complain less? So we have done some research on that. And I do think there's a relationship there. But I think it's really mediated by mindfulness.

14
00:08:14.000 --> 00:09:01.000
And I think that's, to your point earlier, about being an effective complainer, I think it all boils down to awareness. I think the more aware we are of our complaining, so to use the mindfulness terms, the more mindful we are about our own complaining, then the more effective we're going to be. And if we're an effective complainer, and whether for expressive reasons or for instrumental reasons, then we're going to get the improvement in our affect and then we're going to be happier. So I think it's that awareness, mindfulness link. And, you know, it's interesting because there is a Will Bowen. He's a motivational speaker, but he talks about complaining. And he started this movement originally. And he had people like the Livestrong bracelets.

15
00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:38.000
He had people wear these little purple bracelets and it had the complaint-free world, which was his organization. And, you know, he predicated all this on the idea that it takes 21 days to make or break a habit and with the idea that complaining is a habit. And, you know, while I don't share all of his ideas, the premise of, you know, okay, if I complain, I need to move it to the other wrist and start by 21 day period over. I think it's all, I think that works because of the mindfulness, because of the awareness. You know, if I'm having to take the bracelet off and move it, and my next door neighbor is a mental health counselor. And in her prior clinic, one of her therapists used it in marriage counseling.

16
00:09:38.000 --> 00:10:06.000
And so if you started complaining about your spouse, then you had to take the bracelet off and move it to the other wrist. And again, it's awareness. You know, when I first started doing research in this area, oh, my gosh, everybody was complaining. It was like it was coming out of the woodwork. No, they weren't complaining anymore than they had been, but now I was aware of it. And not only was I aware of how much they were complaining. I was also aware of how much I was complaining. But you can't modify it if you're not aware of it.

17
00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:29.000
And what got you interested in doing research into complaining? That's a great story. So I was looking for my own research niche. You know, I was still a few years out of grad school and I was still doing what my advisors had done and I wanted my own path. So I was talking to one of my former advisors and he said, well, you're such a good complainer. Why don't you study that?

18
00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:58.000
Now, I'm sure he meant that facetiously. But that's what we're going to say anyway. I'm going to stick to that story. But, you know, I thought, huh, that's a good idea. So I started getting in the literature and at that time there was only one. one study in psychology that had been conducted on complaining. And it was by somebody that I knew. And for him, it was just a sort of fly-by-night study. So I was like, wow, now, what in my own research niche? I've got, you know, the world is my oyster in this case. And I loved it. And it's

