been many years that I've been involved in this field. And I find it just as exciting now as I did when I first started it. I think it's because it's a behavior that every single person engages in. And speaking of that, is it just human nature to focus on the negative? Is that why we complain? And are there some personality traits that make people more or less prone to be complainers? So I'm going to answer that by saying, I think we have a distorted perception of our own complaining. You know, we've done some research where we had people evaluate their own complaining and other people's complaining. And they perceive their own complaining much more positively than they perceive. other people's complaining. Of course. And so I think that either we're not identifying our complaints as complaints because complaining has such a pejorative tone to it,

20
00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:25.000
or we're interpreting it as other people would identify it as complaining, but we're just thinking of it as confiding, particularly if it's expressive complaining. So we're putting a positive slant on it. So I think there's this almost misattribution of our own behavior relative to the people's behavior. But I think you're right. I think we do have this tendency, pessimist, more than optimist, obviously. People with, you know, different types of locus of control, people with a higher propensity to complain. I do think there are certain people that tend to be

21
00:12:25.000 --> 00:13:10.000
more likely to focus on the negative and more likely to express it. You were quoted in a recent Washington Post piece about the department that handles all the complaints that come into the Washington, D.C. public transit system. That got me thinking, what's the emotional cost of being on the receiving end of constant complaining, especially for people who do it for a living like customer service reps? You know, I've thought about that a lot because I've been contacted by companies before to talk about that. And I think the cost can be high. You know, if that's all you're doing is listening to people's complaints. And I got a letter one time from somebody that said that they were getting ready to give up a very high-paying job and it involved listening to people's complaints for the company.

22
00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:55.000
because they couldn't do it anymore. They couldn't listen to people's complaints. I think it takes a certain kind of person who can be that listener. I'm not sure exactly what the characteristics of that person are. I wish I could figure that out. But I think it genuinely, you know, some people just don't have the patience for it. Some people are, you know, just going to be like, I'll just get over it. Yeah, that would be me. Me too. That's exactly what I would say. But I think it takes a certain kind of person. But I think people have to know that before they go into that kind of position. Because if you don't respond, quote unquote, correctly, then you're just going to set the person who's complaining up to complain more. So what is the correct way to respond to a complaint?

23
00:13:55.000 --> 00:14:36.000
So I think what you do is, you know, it depends if you gauge that the other person is complaining for cathartic reasons. I think you give them their space short term. And then I think you try to redirect it. And, you know, maybe if my neighbor's complaining about the cable company, then I think you let them complain, get it off their chest. And if they keep on and on and on about it, then the redirect is, well, have you called the company? Have you discussed this with them? Have you gone online and expressed your complaint? So I think there's a redirect that becomes necessary. And if it ultimately continues, then I think you have to tell them, maybe complain yourself, you know, it bothers me when you continue to complain so much, because I feel like you're not doing

