And I just, I would love to do that study to see if that's the case. Yeah. And just to wind up, what are you working on now? Where are the big issues that you're looking into the questions you want to answer? So, well, that cross-cultural thing is one of them. bit a perpetual one for me. We just had a study published just a few months ago, and it was looking at everyday versus retail complaining. And, you know, there's a fair amount of research on retail complaining, but there's really not a ton on everyday complaining. And that's the

42
00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:45.000
direction I want to go with it. You know, I want to, I mean, we know the functions that it serves. We know what some of the outcomes are for relationships. But I really want to get it more of the nitty-gritty about everyday complaining. I'd like to get it more of the affect. benefits and cost off. But we know the global perspective. Yeah, it makes you feel worse initially because you're thinking about your dissatisfaction. But if you do it effectively, it makes you feel better. But I would love to do some longitudinal research looking at people's everyday complaints. Well, Dr. Kowalski, I want to thank you for joining me today. This has been fun. It has been fun. Thank you so much for having me.

